Then he explained what the people of the Jahiliyya used to eat, saying: ﴿Forbidden to you is carrion﴾, that is, an animal that dies of its own accord (ḥatf anfihā); ﴿and blood﴾, that is, flowing blood (al-masfūḥ), which is that which is poured forth; ﴿and the flesh of swine﴾, and all of it is impure, and only the flesh is singled out because it is the main object of concern; ﴿and that over which a name other than God's has been invoked﴾, that is, a voice has been raised with it for other than God, namely their saying, "In the name of al-Lat and al-'Uzza," at its slaughter; ﴿and the strangled﴾, those they strangled until they died, or that which strangled itself in a snare or otherwise;

﴿and the beaten to death﴾, those they struck with a stick or a stone until they died;

﴿and the fallen to death﴾, those that fell from a mountain or into a well and died;

﴿and the gored﴾, the one gored, that is, the one another animal butted so that it died from the goring;

﴿and that of which a wild beast has eaten﴾, part of it, and it died from its wound;

﴿except what you have slaughtered﴾, except what you have reached while its slaughter (dhakāh) is still possible, and it is still convulsing in the manner of a slaughtered animal. The exception refers to the strangled animal and what follows it, for if one reaches it while it still has life in it, slaughters it, and mentions God's name over it, it becomes lawful;

﴿and that which is sacrificed on the altars﴾. They had stones set up around the House, upon which they slaughtered, thereby honouring them and seeking nearness through them. (p-426) They are called al-anṣāb (the altars); its singular is nuṣub, or it is a plural whose singular is niṣāb;

﴿and that you seek to divide by arrows﴾. It is in a place of nominative case by conjunction with carrion, meaning: carrion is forbidden to you, and so-and-so. Seeking division by arrows (al-istiqsām bil-azlām) refers to the al-azlām, which are the marked arrow-shafts, singular zalam or zulam. When one of them wished to travel, or to raid, or to trade, or to marry, or for other matters, he would take three arrow-shafts: on one was written, "My Lord has commanded me"; on another, "He has forbidden me"; and the third was blank. If the commanding one came out, he went forth for his need; if the forbidding one came out, he refrained; and if the blank one came out, he drew again. Thus the meaning of seeking division by arrows is seeking knowledge of what has been apportioned to him, of what has not been apportioned to him, by means of the arrows. Al-Zajjaj said: there is no difference between this and the words of the astrologers: "Do not go out because of such-and-such a star; go out at the rising of such-and-such a star." But the Sharḥ al-Ta'wilat refutes this and says: the astrologer does not say that such-and-such a star commands such-and-such, and such-and-such a star forbids such-and-such, as those people did; rather the astrologer makes the stars indications and signs of God's rulings. It is permissible for God to place in the stars meanings and indicators by which the rulings are perceived and things are derived, and there is no blame in that; the blame lies only in judging about God and testifying on His behalf. It has also been said that it is gambling (al-maysir), and their dividing of the slaughtered camel into the known shares.

﴿That is defiance﴾. Seeking division by arrows is departure from obedience. It may be that the reference returns to every prohibition in the verse. ﴿Today﴾ is an adverbial complement of "despaired." It does not mean a specific day; its meaning is "now." This is like your saying, "I am now (al-yawm) old," meaning "now." It has also been said that what is meant is the day of its revelation; it was revealed on Friday, on the Day of 'Arafa after the 'Asr prayer, during the Farewell Pilgrimage.

﴿The disbelievers have despaired of your religion﴾: they despaired of nullifying it, or they despaired of your religion that they would overcome it, because God, exalted is He, fulfilled His promise of making it prevail over all religion. ﴿So do not fear them﴾ after the religion has been made manifest and fear of the disbelievers has disappeared, and they have been turned into the defeated after having been the victors. (And fear Me) without a yā' in connection or pause, meaning: devote fear exclusively to Me. ﴿Today﴾ is an adverbial complement of His saying: ﴿I have perfected for you your religion﴾, by My sufficing you against the fear of your enemy and making you prevail over them, as kings say, "Today our rule has been completed," meaning: we have been sufficed against whom we used to fear. Or: I have perfected for you what you need in your obligations regarding teaching the lawful and the unlawful, and specifying the Islamic laws and the principles of analogical reasoning.

﴿And I have completed My favour upon you﴾ by the conquest of Mecca, your entering it safe and dominant, the demolition of the Jahiliyya and its rites.

﴿And I have approved Islam for you as a religion﴾: a circumstantial clause. I chose it for you from among the religions, and I informed you that it is the religion alone that is approved.

﴿And whoever seeks other than Islam as a religion, it will not be accepted from him﴾ [Āl ʿImrān: 85]. ﴿But whoever is compelled﴾ is connected to the mention of the prohibited things. His saying ﴿That is defiance﴾ is a parenthetical insertion by which the meaning of prohibition is emphasized. Likewise what follows it, because the prohibition of these foul things is part of the complete religion, the perfect favour, and Islam described as approved, not other than it among the creeds. Its meaning: whoever is compelled to carrion, or to other things, ﴿in a state of famine﴾, hunger, ﴿without inclining﴾, a circumstantial clause, ﴿toward sin﴾, leaning toward sin, that is, not exceeding the bounds of staving off hunger. ﴿Then God is Forgiving, Merciful﴾, He does not hold him accountable for that. ﴿Merciful﴾ by permitting the forbidden to the one who has an excuse.

The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger, meaning: the friends of Allah. In the hadith: "Allah, the Exalted, says: Whoever shows contempt for a friend of Mine, has declared war on Me."

﴿And strive upon the earth [to cause] corruption﴾, meaning: as corrupters. It is permissible for ﴿corruption﴾ (فَسادًا) to be a maf'ul lahu (an object of purpose, indicating the reason for the action), meaning: for the sake of corruption. The khabar (predicate) of "جزاء" (recompense) is ﴿that they be killed﴾ and what is conjoined to it. The doubling of the consonant in ﴿yuqattalu﴾ (يُقَتَّلُوا) indicates intensification, applied one after another. Its meaning is:

﴿that they be killed﴾ without crucifixion, if they committed only murder;
﴿or be crucified﴾ along with being killed, if they combined murder with taking property;
﴿or have their hands and feet cut off﴾, if they took property;
﴿from opposite sides﴾ is a hal (circumstantial clause) describing the hands and feet, meaning: differing (i.e., the right hand with the left foot, or vice versa).

﴿Or be banished from the land﴾ by imprisonment, if they did not go beyond causing terror.

﴿That﴾ mentioned punishment ﴿is for them a disgrace in this world﴾, meaning: humiliation and exposure. ﴿And for them in the Hereafter is a great punishment.﴾
